WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.840
Four months before Steve Jobs planned&nbsp;
to reveal the iPhone to the world,

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:09.720
he decided to switch the screen from plastic to&nbsp;
glass, because plastic was too easily scratched.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.120
Engineers freaked out because they didn’t have

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.760
a glass prototype that could&nbsp;
pass the one-meter drop test.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.240
There was, however, an American glassware&nbsp;
company that had once prototyped a strong,

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.000
scratch-proof product dubbed “gorilla&nbsp;
glass” but didn’t have a market for it.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.400
Jobs doubted the glass was good enough and started

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.400
lecturing Corning CEO Wendell&nbsp;
Weeks on how glass was made.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:36.160
Weeks boldly interjected, as described in&nbsp;
Walter Issaacson’s book: “Can you shut up,

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.600
and let me teach you some science?”

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.000
Jobs was convinced. But Weeks said&nbsp;
his company didn’t have the capacity

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.400
to make the gorilla glass in its&nbsp;
factories, to which Jobs said:

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.760
“Don’t be afraid. Yes, you can do it.&nbsp;
Get your mind around it. You can do it.”

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.600
And Corning did do it.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:59.720
The day the iPhone came out, Jobs sent Weeks this&nbsp;
message: “We couldn’t have done it without you.”

